it was supposed to be the summit that  would stop global warming but now  there's deep disappointment with  environmentalists saying cop 26 fell far  short of the urgent action that's needed  negotiations in glasgow went on beyond  the deadline as draft after draft came  closer to producing a global agreement  to restrict greenhouse gases but the  final document was met with anger after  india scuppered its key provision to  phase out coal power  the cobb 26 summit began with hopes to  save the planet and ended with an  apology  i apologize for  the way this process has unfolded  and  i'm deeply sorry  i also understand the the deep  disappointment  but i think as you have noted  it's also vital that we  protect this package  delegates did manage to come to an  agreement at the end of the conference  which has been seen as a final hour  last-ditch effort to stop catastrophic  climate change  most countries agreed on new and more  ambitious targets for reducing emissions  but the pledges fell short of what  science says is needed to stop the world  from heating beyond a dangerous 1.5  degrees celsius  india insisted on a last minute watering  down of the final text changing  references of a coal phase out to a  phase down  and wealthy nations resisted calls to  create a mechanism to compensate poor  countries suffering the worst effects of  climate change  un secretary general antonio guterres  urged countries to do more  as i said at the opening we must  accelerate action to keep the 1.5 degree  goal alive  our fragile planet is hanging by a  thread we are still knocking on the door  of climate catastrophe  it's time to go into emergency mode  or our chance of reaching net zero will  itself be zero  leaders agreed to reconvene for talks in  egypt next year where major emitters  will be asked to present new targets  but with the goal of 1.5 degrees now on  life support activists say cop 27 is  already dead  well deepai and basu ray climate  engagement initiative lead at climate  outreach is joining me now from oxford  in the united kingdom welcome to dw now  you were in glasgow following the  negotiations how disappointed are you  with this agreement  um i mean i think look at the at the  very outset uh the there's kind of the  main point is that this is a this is a  negotiation of of what 197 countries  uh no one you know one of the  delegations one of the southern  delegations said to me no one country  will ever get everything that they want  um  it does however feel very much like a  number of compromises were made in good  faith uh but they weren't reciprocated  with with uh with with similar good  faith on the other sides um so i think  we're all uh disappointed in the lack of  ambition especially as we started the  conference with so many calls  um for high ambition um so i think  there's still an awful lot of work to be  done you know i mean  we've not yet reached the point of  congratulating ourselves on on on on  anything really we've still got all of  the work still to do  diploma how is it possible that the  agreement on coal was watered down  at so last minute  i think there's a couple of points here  um to keep in mind uh and and you know  this links back to the work that climate  outreach does on on kind of public  engagement  um on the actual issue of the watering  down um let's look at it in in it's in  its entirety the there is a phasing down  of coal  yes which is not at all what we would  have uh what what the world needs to  achieve a 1.5 degree world um but the  text around fossil fuels was even more  dubious which was simply  of of of looking to cut out inefficient  subsidies not cutting down  on fossil fuels but cutting down on  inefficient subsidies so there's a lot  of word playing and a lot of gaming  being done by all uh you know all of the  bigger emitters um who are  at the table here and it's very easy to  point fingers at countries like india  and china  because the vast majority of their  energy  sources or rather a large portion of  their energy sources comes from coal  whereas we in the west  are the biggest emitters through fossil  fuels so it's very easy to say well it's  because of this and it's because of this  but there's been a lot of linguistic  gymnastics here uh and and i think the  the the blame needs to be understood in  a much more  uh nuanced way  uh tell me what does the glasgow climate  pact now mean for your future work  um well so i mean i think one thing is  it has been very clear is that  governments particularly those in the in  the in the bigger emitter countries  uh turned up with a set of policies um  that is not necessarily in line with how  their public  views action on climate uh engagement  and and on how climate uh issues need to  be dealt with um and and that is the  work that climate outreach has been  doing consistently for the last uh two  decades which is to try and make sure  and and to engage the public on these  issues you know all of the polls that  are coming out um certainly here in the  uk in the u.s and so on there is an  increased um understanding of of what  climate change is and people are are are  anxious to make  the right changes um in in in their  lives and lifestyles but they don't feel  connected to the policy-making process  so there is a real kind of need for  engagement uh government engagement with  the public on on both sensitizing and  learning and co-creating the solutions  that we need to reach the 1.5 world  dipayan basu ray climate engagement  initiative lead at climate outreach  thank you for joining us on dw today  thank you very much  all right for some analysis on this i'm  joined now by dw correspondent namisha  jaiswal in delhi hi namisha good to see  you now india openly tried to water down  this uh coal resolution did the indian  government get what it wanted in the end  though  well pablo from the indian perspective  it's not so much about simply getting  what it wants but forcing developed  countries to commit to what they owe to  the rest of the world india has insisted  that they are making commitments despite  serious developmental challenges prime  minister narendra modi in his speech  said that india will cut down emissions  it will cut to net zero emissions by  2070 and convert to renewable sources of  energy half of its energy by 2030 india  says that this shows its commitment to  mitigating climate change despite the  challenges it faces as a developing  country but it is entitled to  responsible use of coal which is why the  language watering down was pushed for  right now rich nations have been  reluctant to agree any mechanism to  provide funding for loss and damage what  does that mean for india  well this is definitely caused and  contributed to a trust deficit  especially when the hundred billion  dollar commitment to mitigate climate  change in developing countries was  missed in 2020 india insists that  develop developed countries grew as a  consequence of their heavy reliance on  coal and that these developing countries  understanding with climate changes  should be allowed to use coal  responsibly simply put it's a case of  alleging that the developed countries  are having their cake and eating it too  that they grew over 70 or 80 years  depending on coal but now are insisting  that india and other developing  countries need to be doing more by  committing to cuts in carbon emissions  and this is causing the resentment and  pushback that we're seeing however india  insists that it's doing more than it can  by committing to these goals  we're seeing some images there on the  screen of uh some of the pollution in  india how how urgent is it for india  though to do more than what's been  agreed on in the glasgow climate pact  well that's right pablo nobody can say  that india is not facing the  consequences of climate change i'm  sitting here in delhi and feeling a  trickle in my throat because my air  purifier is not on we've seen extreme  climatic events around the country  floods and cyclones  there's not only loss of life and  property but there could also be  potential loss of land as land is  getting submerged as a consequence of  climate change by some estimates if we  miss the 1.5 degree coal even in cities  like delhi being outside for three or  four hours as time as the temperature  increases could prove to be fatal so  there are definitely consequences that  the indian population could face however  the indian government almost has to  negotiate a trade-off india has suffered  massively consequence of coronavirus  lockdowns and it's a question of can we  trade how much of today can we trade for  the future  let me show you as well ian delhi thank  you
 

